49.3,2.4],"to":[48.95,2.35],"side":"B"}},{"date":"1940-06-17","text":"Marshal Pétain, France's new head of government, asks Germany for armistice terms.","arrow":null},{"date":"1940-06-22","text":"France signs an armistice at Compiègne. It takes effect on 25 June.","arrow":null}],"short_center":[3.2,48.3],"long_bbox":[-5.2,43.4,9.2,54.0],"short_bbox":[-1.8,42.8,8.2,54.2],"thumb_date":"1940-05-20","thumb_text":"Fall of France","parts":{"b":{"from":"1940-05-10","to":"1940-06-04","title":"26 Days from the Ardennes to Dunkirk","subtitle":"The German breakthrough, May 1940"}},"yt":{"long_title":"Fall of France 1940: Every Day on the Map (May - June 1940)","short_a_title":"France fell in just six weeks. Every day on the map 🗺️","short_b_title":"How the panzers trapped the Allies at Dunkirk, day by day 🗺️","about":"On 10 May 1940 Germany invaded the Netherlands, Belgium and Luxembourg. While the Allies moved north into Belgium, German armour came through the Ardennes and crossed the Meuse at Sedan and Dinant on 13 May. The Dutch army capitulated on 15 May, and on 20 May German tanks reached the Channel near Abbeville, cutting off the Allied armies in the north. Belgium surrendered on 28 May, and from 26 May to 4 June about 338,000 Allied soldiers were evacuated from Dunkirk. On 5 June Germany attacked across the Somme. Paris was entered on 14 June, Marshal Pétain asked for terms on 17 June, and the armistice signed at Compiègne on 22 June took effect on 25 June.","tags":["Battle of France","Fall of France","1940","Dunkirk","Blitzkrieg","WW2","World War 2","Western Front","history map","day by day","front line map","Sedan"],"hashtag":"ww2"}}